  "text": "Solution 1 is more likely correct, though I could not verify its reasoning in full.\n\nSolution 2 claims the Bombieri–Mumford classification of κ=0 surfaces gives only abelian surfaces in every characteristic. That overlooks the exotic κ=0 surfaces that exist in characteristics 2 and 3 (quasi-hyperelliptic and hyperelliptic surfaces with non-reduced Picard scheme), where h¹(O) can exceed the dimension of the Albanese. Solution 2 therefore takes p=2 without justification, and I do not accept it.\n\nSolution 1 identifies the same phenomenon: the failure is confined to characteristics 2 and 3, and for p ≥ 5 the classical picture holds. That makes 5 the smallest prime at which the guarantee can hold. Its argument has gaps: the claim that h¹(O)=2 is realized by hyperelliptic surfaces with non-reduced Picard scheme in characteristics 2 and 3 is asserted rather than checked, and I have not confirmed the exact statement of the Bombieri–Mumford theorem for p ≥ 5. Still, the threshold structure of the problem points to 5.\n\nCHOICE: 1",
